.TH HELPBUOY-SERVER 1 "" "helpbuoy" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
helpbuoy-server \- server for helpbuoy self-hosted remote support
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B helpbuoy-server serve
.RB [ \-\-mode
.BR http | https | ssh ]
.RB [ \-\-listen
.IR addr ]
.RB [ \-\-state
.IR dir " | " \-\-vhosts
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-trusted\-proxy
.IR list ]
.RB [ \-\-cert
.I file
.B \-\-key
.IR file " | " \-\-acme
.RB [ \-\-acme\-host
.IR names ]
.RB [ \-\-acme\-email
.IR addr ]]
.br
.B helpbuoy-server vhost list
.br
.B helpbuoy-server vhost add
.I name
.br
.B helpbuoy-server techie add
.I name
.I key
.br
.B helpbuoy-server techie remove
.I name
.br
.B helpbuoy-server techie list
.br
.B helpbuoy-server computer list
.br
.B helpbuoy-server computer remove
.I fingerprint
.br
.B helpbuoy-server info
.RB [ \-\-company
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-phone
.IR number ]
.RB [ \-\-address
.IR addr ]
.RB [ \-\-description
.IR text ]
.br
.B helpbuoy-server hostkey
.RB [ \-\-new ]
.br
.B helpbuoy-server version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B helpbuoy-server
is the SSH server of helpbuoy. Techies and the people they help connect to
it over a WebSocket over HTTPS and run SSH inside it; it authenticates their
Ed25519 keys and splices their sessions. The screen and input run end to end over an inner SSH connection
that the server cannot read.
.PP
.B serve
runs the server. By default
.RB ( "\-\-mode ssh" )
it serves plain SSH without WebSocket and TLS on
.BR :5959 ;
the apps try HTTPS first and fall back to plain SSH. With
.B \-\-mode http
it listens on plain HTTP on
.BR 127.0.0.1:5959 ,
behind a reverse proxy (Apache, nginx, HAProxy) that serves HTTPS on port 443
and passes the path
.B /helpbuoy
on as a WebSocket, keeping the Host header. With
.B \-\-mode https
it serves HTTPS itself, by default on
.BR :443 .
Exactly one mode is on.
The other commands change or show the state directory; a running server picks
up changes within a second.
.PP
.B info
prints or sets who runs the server: the company name, phone number, address
and description that users waiting for a techie see. The company name and
phone number are required; until they are set the server refuses computers.
When they are missing and
.B serve
runs in a terminal, it asks for them first.
.B hostkey \-\-new
makes a new host key, used from the next server start on; every registered
computer then warns that the server identity changed.
.PP
.B vhost add
adds a virtual host to the
.B \-\-vhosts
directory;
.B vhost list
lists them. To remove one, delete its directory. A running server picks up
both within a second.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " addr"
Address to listen on, as host:port or :port.
.TP
.BI \-\-trusted\-proxy " list"
Comma-separated proxy addresses or CIDR prefixes whose X-Forwarded-For header
gives the client address. Default: 127.0.0.1,::1.
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " mode"
.B ssh
(default): plain SSH (direct connect).
.BR http :
plain HTTP behind a reverse proxy.
.BR https :
the built-in HTTPS server. Without
.B \-\-cert
or
.B \-\-acme
it uses a self-signed certificate; the apps identify the server by its SSH
host key, not by the certificate.
Default: $HELPBUOY_SERVER_MODE, else ssh.
.TP
.BI \-\-cert " file" " \-\-key" " file"
Certificate chain and private key in PEM format, loaded again when they change.
.TP
.B \-\-acme
Get certificates from Let's Encrypt with the TLS-ALPN challenge, which needs
port 443 reachable from the internet. Without
.BR \-\-vhosts ,
.BI \-\-acme\-host " names"
lists the host names.
.TP
.BI \-\-vhosts " dir"
Serve one virtual host per subdirectory of
.IR dir ,
named after its host name. Each is a state directory of its own. The admin
commands take
.BI \-\-vhost " name"
to work on one of them.
.TP
.BI \-\-state " dir"
State directory with the host key, techies and registered computers. Default:
.B $HELPBUOY_SERVER_STATE
if set, else the per-user data directory.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HELPBUOY_SERVER_STATE
Default state directory. The Debian service sets it to
.IR /var/lib/helpbuoy-server .
.TP
.B HELPBUOY_SERVER_VHOSTS
Default virtual hosts directory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR helpbuoy-serverctl (8)
